00:02:59.529 --> 00:03:01.820
All right, so now let's talk
about DevOps for a minute.

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:07.280
Some lovely quotes here.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.340
"DevOps is not a goal, but
a never-ending process."

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.000
It is how most of us by now
in the past like three or four

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.610
years are used to working.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:20.300
We want to get stuff-- features
in in the hands of our testers

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.790
or our constituents
in the company

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:26.210
and get iteration faster,
find the bugs faster,

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.410
shorten the loop.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:33.280
And so in this, what
I'm going to demonstrate

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:40.690
is a dogfooding pipeline,
where basically we're

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:44.320
building and testing internally,
developing, pushing stuff

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.390
to a repository,
and then sending out

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:47.530
apps to our dogfooders.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.200
What dogfood means
at Google is sort

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.410
of folks internally testing
our applications before we

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:58.090
release them, which I think is a
fairly common term in industry.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:04.530
And so with mobile, DevOps
is a little bit funky,

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:09.300
because unlike web, we've
got to sign this binary.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.760
We have to create the binary.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:11.950
We have to sign the binary.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:14.409
We have get the
binary to the users,

00:04:14.409 --> 00:04:16.200
and they have to install
it from somewhere.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.310
And there's lots of
extra stuff going

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.200
on there that's not like web.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.970
We can't just build a
new Docker container

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.220
and launch it into Kubernetes.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:30.000
We have to actually
physically deliver

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.790
a binary to the mobile user.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.940
But we can still do that
using this fun new product--

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:45.500
old product-- renamed product
called Cloud Build, which

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:50.240
was originally termed
Container Builder

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.490
and meant to be used to
build Docker containers,

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.660
and maybe deployed into
Kubernetes and things

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.020
like that.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:01.520
It turns out, what we
learned about Cloud Build is

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.580
that we can build
anything, and the community

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.630
taught us that the most.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:10.090
There's a Cloud
Builders repository

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:13.480
which I'll show you that
has many different types

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.930
of builders for Cloud Build.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:18.450
But back to Mobile DevOps.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:23.750
So this is a flow
for Mobile DevOps.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:27.250
We trigger some build.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:29.170
We test the build.

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:31.570
At least, a smoke
test on the build.

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:36.040
And distribute that
binary, and then maybe we

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:39.010
monitor for crashing
or other analytics.

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.090
So it starts with developers.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.710
Hopefully it starts
with a repository

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:47.960
we're all putting our code in.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:53.150
I think that's a pretty
known commodity nowadays,

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:56.090
but if you're not using
a source code repository,

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:59.580
you probably should
look into that.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.770
And then we're going to
trigger a Cloud Build process.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:07.135
And these are the repositories
currently supported

00:06:07.135 --> 00:06:07.760
by Cloud Build.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:13.250
You can use Google Cloud Source
Repo, which I'm using here--

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.210
GitHub, or BitBucket.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:20.330
This is what it looks like
to create a Build trigger.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:22.100
There's basically three steps.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:27.080
You pick out the Source
Control Repo you're using,

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.155
you select the
specific repository,

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:30.530
and then you set
up your trigger.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:37.050
When you set up your trigger,
you can pick out a branch.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:43.710
You can obviously name your
trigger, pick out a branch,

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:44.710
or a tag.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:47.360
So you can trigger based on tag.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.240
I'm using a branch here
kind of a la GitFlow,

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:54.240
but you could just as easily
use a tag, which might actually

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.577
be a little bit better
for your specific case.

00:06:57.577 --> 00:06:59.160
And this is how you
trigger the build,

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.201
which is what I just did.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:09.660
So building the binary,
again we were triggering

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:13.680
a build in Container Builder--

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.080
or Cloud Build, rather.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:17.600
I'm going to mess that up a lot.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:25.420
And that build is described by
this funky magic file called

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:28.090
cloudbuild.yaml.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:32.080
And that is what describes every
step that you want to take,

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:34.570
just like you would in your--

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:37.247
if you're in Jenkins, then
you have list of steps.

00:07:37.247 --> 00:07:38.080
You would have that.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:41.320
This is the description
of the list of steps

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:47.320
that the Cloud Build will take
in order to build your binary

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:49.960
and test it and do whatever
else you want to do.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:08:01.120
And in this case, we're
going to push source code.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.790
And the steps are
going to be described

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:05.590
in the cloudbuild.yaml.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:09.310
And the results
of our Cloud Build

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:13.660
is going to be a distribution
through Fabric or Crashlytics

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:17.410
data, which is going to
end up emailing our users

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.810
about the new application.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:24.830
That slide didn't show up right.

00:08:28.281 --> 00:08:30.260
Sorry.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:31.680
OK.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.400
So the Cloud Build
workflow is again,

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:39.480
a bunch of steps that
operate on your workspace.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.299
So when that trigger
happens, Cloud Build

00:08:42.299 --> 00:08:45.180
pulls the source
code and drops in all

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.180
in a workspace directory,
just like Jenkins.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.990
That probably sounds familiar
to anybody who's used Jenkins.

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:55.350
And then it runs a
bunch of build steps.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:58.380
The thing that's interesting
about it is that all of those

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.930
build steps are containers.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:05.930
And the commands they're
in are contained.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:08.820
All of their dependencies are
contained in Docker containers

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:09.780
and images.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:14.850
And so in this case, we're
doing a gsutil, copy stuff

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:18.870
from Cloud Storage,
an Android build,

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:23.340
another test using
the gcloud command,

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:24.900
and some more Android build.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:31.530
Each one of those steps is
its own Docker container.

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.050
So you're actually calling
Docker Run in order

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.060
to complete that step
in the workspace.

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:47.300
And like I said before, there's
a rich set of those containers

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:50.340
in the community site.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:52.370
You can find stuff--

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:55.470
the Android container is
the one I'm discussing here,

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:56.960
which is contributed by me.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:01.340
But it's open source, so
if you have edits and PRs,

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:03.440
you can issue PRs to it.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.330
But there's, as
you can see, a ton

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:10.860
of different containers for
doing builds and deployments.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:14.684
There's one for, for
instance, create gcloud.

00:10:14.684 --> 00:10:16.850
You can even create compute
instances and everything

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.350
like that.

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:20.060
There's one for
kubectl if you're

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:24.890
deploying a Kubernetes
manifest, deploying a Kubernetes

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.630
infrastructure.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:28.950
And there's one
there that's called

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:31.430
Dataflow Python, which
as you can imagine,

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:36.930
is meant for setting
up a Dataflow job.

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:40.140
So there's just lots
of possibilities.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.640
So in using the Android
Builder, what you're going to do

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:49.960
is build the container that's
going to run the Android build.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:55.730
So it's going to have the Gradle
and Java SDK and the Android

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:56.929
SDK.

00:10:56.929 --> 00:10:58.970
And in order to build that
up, what we want to do

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:02.570
is we want to build a container
that's relatively lean.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:06.770
So we don't want to necessarily
include every Android

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:11.460
SDK from the beginning of
time to now in that Docker

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:14.540
container, because it would take
a really long time to pull that

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:16.620
from the registry.

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:21.350
So in order to semi-customize
and package an Android builder

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:26.450
for your own needs, you edit
this packages.text file,

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:28.190
which specifies all
the packages you

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:30.610
want in your Android Builder.

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:36.200
And every time you create
a new Android Builder,

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:40.640
you can change the
packages you're using.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:44.450
Now the Android
builder also requires

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:47.990
you to supply your license
key from your workstation.

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:52.400
So in your Android
SDK home, you're

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.710
going to have a
licenses directory,

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:57.050
so you can supply
your Android SDK

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:00.635
license from there as a build
substitution in Container.

00:12:00.635 --> 00:12:02.645
But I'll show you
that in a minute.

00:12:02.645 --> 00:12:06.200
But so each of these would
have to have the EULA already

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:09.800
accepted, and then the license
key hash that you supplied

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:13.190
to Container
Builder will already

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:15.440
know that you have
accepted the license

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:16.940
and it will can
continue to build.

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:23.550
And so after that,
this is the steps.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:26.440
You clone the repo.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:32.750
You create the packages.text
file in that directory,

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:38.440
and then you submit the Android
Builder's cloudbuild.yaml file,

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:41.980
which is going to build a
container in your Google

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:44.760
Cloud's Container Registry.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:47.320
That's going to have the
Android Builder piece,

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:49.960
and you can just go ahead
and call that from your apps

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:52.220
build, which I'll go through.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:58.880
So that's the three steps.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:03.650
And then the result is
you have this Android

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:05.935
Builder in your registry.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:17.340
If you want more information
on the Android Builder and more

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:22.710
specifically, there's a
document drafted by me

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:26.610
on creating this pipeline in
more detail on our website,

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:29.490
so you can hit up
this document and it

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.710
goes through all of the
steps for setting up

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:32.714
the Android Builder.

00:13:32.714 --> 00:13:34.380
So you don't have to
remember everything

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:35.980
that I'm saying here.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:40.610
I'm just trying to give
you an introduction.

00:13:40.610 --> 00:13:43.210
But here's some more
details that you're probably

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:44.380
looking for.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:47.890
So in getting more
detail about what

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:49.930
we need to do to
build the mobile app,

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:53.050
we are going to need to
sign the Android application

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:54.400
with a Keystore file, right?

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.380
Everyone who is an
Android developer

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:58.880
probably is familiar with that.

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:02.920
And so the way we approach
that in this, is we just

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:05.680
go ahead and copy all of
the signing information

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:07.570
to a storage bucket.

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:09.340
And that storage
bucket will only

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:12.950
be accessible from the project
that you're using to build.

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:16.120
And you could secure that even
further with key management,

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:18.610
but that's out of scope here.

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:25.080
You copy your Fabric secrets
up to a different bucket

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:27.670
or the same bucket.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:34.450
And there's an extra little
optimization here called

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:36.130
the build cache,
which sort of tries

00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:39.862
to optimize the dependencies for
Gradle so it's not downloading

00:14:39.862 --> 00:14:42.070
the internet all the way
from the internet, but maybe

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:47.650
downloading the internet closer
to where CloudBuild lives.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:51.770
And then we move on
for our test items.

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:57.880
The other thing to note here
is the arguments in each one.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:01.960
Those are actually arguments
to the Docker run entry point.

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:03.419
And you can change
the entry point.

00:15:03.419 --> 00:15:05.334
None of these actually
change the entry point.

00:15:05.334 --> 00:15:07.750
But you can change the entry
point on these containers

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:12.870
to just sort of execute
arbitrary Bash as well

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:15.160
and still have-- for
instance, let's say

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.950
in the gsutil I want to
just do arbitrary Bash.

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:21.250
I can add an entry point
in there, run that Bash.

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:26.080
But that Bash still has gsutil
in the path for that command.

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:29.830
So that Bash command can
call any of the dependencies

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:33.380
from the gsutil
container, for instance.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:35.440
So this is sort of how
you put together a build.

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:40.450
The other thing to note here
is the substitutions we have.

00:15:40.450 --> 00:15:44.950
Any of these shell variables
with an underscore in the front

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:47.770
is what we call a
substitution in CloudBuild.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:50.380
And what that is, is a
value you supply when you

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:52.750
configure the build trigger.

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:56.050
So you can update,
you can basically

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:57.270
parameterize your build.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:59.920
So the signing API's
secret bucket path

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:02.920
is where to find
fabric.properties

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:04.750
in Cloud Storage.

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:10.840
It's actually a gs://uri.

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:13.780
The license is whatever
you copy and paste it

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:15.120
in for the license.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:16.870
There's a new version
pending, by the way,

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:19.270
where you can copy all
of your Android licenses

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:23.150
to another bucket and use
a bucket reference instead.

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:25.600
That's a PR that's been
submitted and currently

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.760
pending.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:33.580
And then another parameter
to mention here is waitFor.

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:37.370
waitFor is how you
wait for other things.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:39.500
So you need your signing
files, obviously,

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:43.270
before you build
the distribution

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:44.290
version of the app.

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:47.080
And so, you're going to
want to wait for that copy

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:52.920
to complete before you
build the distribution file.

00:17:00.468 --> 00:17:02.770
I'll have to go on
to the next slide

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:06.020
to show more dependencies,
but you get the idea.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:11.900
So after we've built
the binary, in this one

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:13.910
we build the debug
version of the binary.

00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:17.270
And then we're going to
test that debug version

00:17:17.270 --> 00:17:23.270
with Firebase Test Lab, which
is a service that Firebase

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:26.690
provides that has actual
physical devices in a data

00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:30.001
center that you can run both
robot tests and functional

00:17:30.001 --> 00:17:30.500
tests on.

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:33.260
This pipeline specifically
deals with robo tests,

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:39.140
but you can also record and
create Espresso functional

00:17:39.140 --> 00:17:42.570
tests, and run those tests on
the devices in the data center.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:49.730
So it lets you run
detailed tests.

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:56.030
And you can see the results and
add workflow, and et cetera.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:00.040
Oh, yes.

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:08.065
Let's take a look
at the devices.

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:22.420
So I pulled up a list of
devices recently from Test Lab.

00:18:22.420 --> 00:18:26.020
So these are the devices you
can actually run your functional

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:27.410
and robo tests on.

00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:28.949
There's quite a few of them.

00:18:28.949 --> 00:18:30.490
Some of them are
virtual, you'll see.

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:33.420
But most of them,
the vast majority,

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:37.360
are actual physical devices that
your APK will be loaded onto.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:40.360
The debug version of the APK
will be loaded onto and tested,

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:43.450
or the test APK as well
if you're using Espresso.

00:18:49.486 --> 00:18:52.360
Let me switch.

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:55.300
And so, here's our
workflow again.

00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:02.940
OK, so the Test Lab build
step is interesting.

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:11.330
So here is where I had to use
the Bash notion of, I'm running

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:13.580
GCloud, but I'm going
to wrap it in some Bash

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:16.760
to make sure because the
Test Lab command in GCloud

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:21.280
doesn't give me a good
status process exit code.

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:22.740
Wouldn't test pass or fail.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:27.010
So I've added some
fanciness here to check

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:28.410
whether the test passed.

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:31.640
And there's a corrupt
statement further down.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:38.525
But you can see more of the
waitFor up here in the build.

00:19:38.525 --> 00:19:41.252
So you can see some more
of those waitFor arguments.

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:50.500
And this is sort of like some
screenshots from a robo test.

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:54.370
I can actually flip
over to the other laptop

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:57.880
and show you some of the
output of a robo test

00:19:57.880 --> 00:20:00.057
if you're interested.

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:12.860
So here you can see,
this is some of the flow.

00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:18.200
The robo test is kind of like
a random touch and type test.

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:20.780
And you can see I'm
using Firebase Auth.

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:22.230
I know this is a little small.

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:25.490
I can get into some screenshots.

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:29.730
But I'm using
Firebase Auth here,

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:33.095
which uses Google sign-in.

00:20:33.095 --> 00:20:35.870
And actually, this thing
comes up with its own Google

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:40.730
account and signs in to
the app, and then starts

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:45.140
touching different
aspects of the app

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:47.930
just to see if anything crashes.

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:53.360
So let's say this Create
button was enabled in here.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:56.570
Then when the robo test hits
Create with an empty string,

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:58.589
it might have caused
a null pointer.

00:20:58.589 --> 00:21:00.630
And if that happened, the
test would have failed.

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:11.125
And you can also get a video
recording of your robo test

00:21:11.125 --> 00:21:14.710
so you can go through and
see what the automation did.

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:16.350
And this is actually on a Pixel.

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:40.850
So it's getting the OAuth
screen, and then saying yes.

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:45.750
And then now it's got it open.

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:47.550
It's a little flickery
with the video,

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:50.240
but you can see sort of
what different screens

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:54.335
it's accessing and all of that.

00:21:54.335 --> 00:21:58.190
It's sort of like
a blind person just

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:00.200
trying to break your
application, that's

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:04.200
the best way to describe it.

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:09.480
Going to flip back.

00:22:13.180 --> 00:22:19.780
So the rest of the build is
trying to distribute our--

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:23.560
assuming the test pass, assuming
there's no failure in the robo

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:25.540
test, we're going to
want to send it out

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:31.120
to dogfooders for the folks
internally for further usage.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:34.720
And so, that's what Fabric
Crashlytics is used for.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:39.770
And in this case, I'm using
just a Gradle command for that.

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:43.270
You can actually
use fastlane here.

00:22:43.270 --> 00:22:48.250
But Android developers
still like Gradle, I think.

00:22:48.250 --> 00:22:49.840
Some are converting to fastlane.

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:51.580
I'm not really sure.

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:54.890
But this is, suffice to say,
a Gradle implementation.

00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:59.380
So a couple of things to
note in this part of the

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:02.620
build is that we're
wrapping the Gradle

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:06.460
command with a script
that does that fancy grab

00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:09.790
the cached jars from
the storage bucket,

00:23:09.790 --> 00:23:12.955
and it copies the licenses
over that you supply.

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:19.480
The other things you see
here are substitutions.

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:25.330
So the build type is the beta
build type, in this case.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:30.550
And the email distribution group
is the set of folks in Fabric

00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:32.970
where I want to
email the build to.

00:23:32.970 --> 00:23:40.880
And the upload
distribution build

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:43.760
type is the thing
that actually uploads

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:49.670
the APK to Fabric Crashlytics.

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:53.450
And then once that's
completely uploaded,

00:23:53.450 --> 00:23:56.780
your users that are
your testers will

00:23:56.780 --> 00:23:59.030
get an email notifying
them that there has been

00:23:59.030 --> 00:24:03.210
an update to the application.

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:07.770
And so, this is sort of what
that tester UI looks like.

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:11.100
You can add emails for
different applications

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:16.600
you might have, and pick
out which of those groups

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:18.507
you want to email
your results to.

00:24:18.507 --> 00:24:19.840
And you can have different ones.

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:22.214
Maybe you have different build
flavors or different build

00:24:22.214 --> 00:24:25.000
types, depending on
who in the company.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:27.530
You might have some that
just go to developers,

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:30.940
some that go to QA after that,
some that, after that, might

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:32.380
go to product managers.

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:35.500
And then, maybe, finally
the whole company.

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:40.710
So you can really
granularly adjust that.

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:46.350
Finally, what we do here
is this is Crashlytics.

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:49.640
And this is an actual bug
that I had in the code.

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:52.950
It's hard to see stack trace,
the call-in stack trace.

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:58.180
But I had a bug with
my Firebase access.

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:00.880
And this was one of
the notifications I got

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:03.950
from Crashlytics of the crash.

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:07.780
So you can-- once this
thing's out in the wild

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:10.000
or out to your users,
depending on who they are,

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:12.430
you can monitor for crashes.

00:25:12.430 --> 00:25:16.150
So this is sort of
like a full serverless

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:22.150
pipeline where everything is
done using managed services.

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:26.710
There's nothing here that's
sort of an online thing you

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:28.330
have to keep up and running.

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:32.950
There's no Jenkins server,
there's no Kubernetes cluster

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:34.694
to keep up and running.

00:25:34.694 --> 00:25:35.860
There's nothing to maintain.

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:40.150
It's all sort of when you
submit that new CloudBuild YAML,

00:25:40.150 --> 00:25:43.350
it boots up that container
and starts running

00:25:43.350 --> 00:25:44.350
all the Docker commands.

00:25:44.350 --> 00:25:48.550
So that's sort of
the use case here.

00:25:56.410 --> 00:25:59.291
Just a minute.

00:25:59.291 --> 00:25:59.790
All right.

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:02.190
So a few minutes ago
I issued a build,

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:05.870
and we can see the
results of the build.

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:07.410
It was successful.

00:26:07.410 --> 00:26:11.430
And this is sort of what
the output of our pipeline

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:12.900
looks like here.

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:13.960
You can see each command.

00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:20.710
So this is running--

00:26:20.710 --> 00:26:26.790
Ubuntu is just an arbitrary
shell running container.

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:31.370
So if I use Ubuntu, I can just
call like mkdir, or whatever,

00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:33.710
stuff like that.

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:35.300
I've got a copy licenses step.

00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:37.240
And I can just
page through here.

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:38.900
I'm not going to go
through everything.

00:26:38.900 --> 00:26:40.410
The Test Lab one is interesting.

00:26:40.410 --> 00:26:43.550
You can see that this one takes
a bit of time, five minutes,

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:46.310
to complete, which is why
I do that at the beginning,

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:50.240
and then wait until it finishes.

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:53.844
And you can see finally
that at the end of this,

00:26:53.844 --> 00:26:55.010
there's a whole other build.

00:26:55.010 --> 00:27:00.100
And at the end, it finally
sends it up to Fabrics.

00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:02.960
There's actually two Gradle
builds that happen in here.

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:06.190
The first one is a debug
build for the robo test.

00:27:06.190 --> 00:27:10.750
And robo test takes the debug
APK and does its testing on it.

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:13.780
And then finally,
it's push to Fabric.

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:22.820
And then from there I
get an email, hopefully,

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:25.050
if I can get my
beach ball to stop.

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:27.180
There we go.

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:30.730
I should get an
email to my user.

00:27:30.730 --> 00:27:32.209
I'll just go on the--

00:27:42.478 --> 00:27:46.160
Well, I should've
gotten an email here.

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:46.660
Interesting.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:48.160
Maybe I'm sending
it to this one.

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:55.540
OK.

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:07.030
Yeah, so from there, my
